&lt;p data-pm-slice=&quot;1 1 []&quot;&gt;US President Donald Trump on Friday announced a deal under which Russia will supply diesel to American and global markets, hailing the arrangement as a way to bring down fuel prices ahead of the November 3 midterm elections. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy, however, has criticised the move, calling it a &amp;ldquo;gift to Putin&amp;rdquo; and warning that easing sanctions without a lasting de-escalation agreement could help Moscow prolong its war against Ukraine.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Trump said that Russian President Vladimir Putin had agreed to immediately supply more than 300,000 metric tonnes of diesel, equivalent to around 2.25 million barrels of fuel. The US administration has also temporarily lifted sanctions on Russian fuel, restrictions originally intended to deprive Moscow of revenue for its war in Ukraine.&lt;/p&gt;
